# REGRESSION — seeding an `on error:` handler must contribute its USES
# only, never its KILLS. Walking the handler into the caller's live set
# deletes a name the normal path still reads: ICE rc 101 on both lanes.
#
# The liveness walker is UNDER-APPROXIMATION-CRITICAL: a use it cannot
# see makes a variable look dead at an earlier consuming position, so
# the value is MOVED instead of cloned and the later read finds freed
# memory. RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into
# the vector. Without it the moved-from read still finds live memory.
# PROBE K1 -- the SEED applies KILLS, not just uses. `seed_on_error_uses`
# (liveness.rs:82-90) calls `walk_block` on the handler body with the CALLER's
# live set, and `walk_block` applies kills as well as uses. At the top level
# that set starts EMPTY, so the kill is harmless; inside a nested block it is
# the enclosing block's live-out, so a kill inside a handler registered there
# deletes a name live on the NORMAL path -- the exact leak `606a7c4e`
# fixed for
# the un-nested shape.
#
# A NAMED SCOPE is the wrapper because its arm passes `live` straight
# through (the straight-line-scope arm in `walk_stmt`, `liveness.rs:342`);
# `if` unions with `saved` and masks it. The nested handler is DISCOVERED
# through the named-scope alternative in `on_error_bodies_in`
# (`liveness.rs:143`) — the same arm, so the D50 rename of `unsafe:` to a
# named scope changed no liveness path here.
#
# ⚠ RED-VERIFY STATUS, MEASURED 2026-08-29 AND NOT REPRODUCIBLE LOCALLY.
# Two targeted breaks of the mechanism this header names both leave this
# fixture GREEN: (1) making `walk_stmt`'s straight-line-scope arm a no-op
# (`Stmt::NamedScope { .. } => {}`), and (2) regressing `seed_on_error_uses`
# to pass the caller's `live` straight to `walk_block`, i.e. re-applying the
# handler's KILLS — the exact defect the fixture is named for. Neither reds.
# The reason is visible in the program: the handler ASSIGNS to `s` and reads
# nothing, so the seed contributes no uses, and the seeded kill is undone by
# the reverse walk reaching `print(s)` first. Its sibling
# `liveness_use_inside_named_scope.gg` DOES red under break (1) (ICE rc 101,
# `local _N read after MoveZero`), so the arm itself is guarded there.
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE ACTUALLY PINS TODAY: the end-to-end shape — a handler
# registered inside a nested scope must not corrupt the normal path — against
# the original `606a7c4e`-era defect. Treat it as a shape pin, not as a
# discriminating guard for the two mechanisms above, until someone builds a
# variant whose handler READS a name the normal path also reads.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into the
# vector.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f() throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    guarded:
        on error:
            s = mk("c", "d")
    print(s)
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f()! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
